data.content:
Here's the refactored test using TUnit's native `ThrowsExactly` assertion with a chained message check:

```csharp
using TUnit.Core;

public class OrderProcessorTests
{
    [Test]
    public async Task ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsInvalidOperation()
    {
        var processor = new OrderProcessor();

        var exception = await Assert.ThrowsAsync<InvalidOperationException>(
            () => processor.ProcessOrder(new Order()));

        await Assert.That(exception.Message).IsEqualTo("Order must contain at least one item");
    }
}
```

This uses `Assert.ThrowsAsync<T>` to verify the exception type is thrown and returns the exception instance, then asserts on the message separately. If you want it all in a single expression, TUnit also supports:

```csharp
await Assert.That(() => processor.ProcessOrder(new Order()))
    .ThrowsException()
    .OfType<InvalidOperationException>()
    .WithMessage("Order must contain at least one item");
```

This second form is fully idiomatic TUnit — one fluent expression that verifies both type and message.
